3.What are Singleton, Scoped, and Transient?

   ASP.NET Core DI Lifetimes

		1. Transient

		- A new instance is created every time the service is requested.
		- Used for lightweight and stateless services.
		- Each class receives a different object.

		Example:

		builder.Services.AddTransient<IEmailService, EmailService>();

		Example:
		Controller
			|
			|---- EmailService (Object 1)
			|
			|---- EmailService (Object 2)

		service1 != service2

		Real-time examples:
		- Email sender
		- PDF generator
		- File converter
		- QR code generator

		Interview Answer:
		"Transient creates a new instance every time it is requested."


--------------------------------------------------


		2. Scoped

		- One instance is created per HTTP request.
		- All services inside the same request share the same instance.
		- A new instance is created for the next request.

		Example:

		builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();

		Request 1:

		Controller
			|
		Service
			|
		Repository
			|
		Same DbContext instance


		Request 2:

		New DbContext instance


		Real-time example:

		E-commerce Order Flow:

		Controller
			|
		OrderService
			|
		PaymentService
			|
		InventoryService
			|
		Repository


		All use the same DbContext during one request.

		Why DbContext is Scoped?

		One Request
			  |
		One Transaction
			  |
		One DbContext


		Interview Answer:
		"Scoped creates one instance per HTTP request and is mainly used for DbContext because it maintains consistency within a request."


--------------------------------------------------


		3. Singleton

		- Only one instance is created for the entire application.
		- The same object is shared by all users and requests.
		- Object exists until the application stops.

		Example:

		builder.Services.AddSingleton<ICacheService, CacheService>();

		Application Starts
				|
		Create Object
				|
		All Users Share Same Object
				|
		Application Stops


		Real-time examples:
		- Memory Cache
		- Configuration Service
		- Application Settings


		Interview Answer:
		"Singleton creates a single instance for the entire application lifetime and shares it with all users."


		--------------------------------------------------


		DI Lifetime Comparison

		Transient:
		- New object every time
		- Short lifetime
		- Stateless services


		Scoped:
		- One object per HTTP request
		- Request lifetime
		- DbContext


		Singleton:
		- One object for entire application
		- Application lifetime
		- Cache and configuration


		Easy Way to Remember:

		Transient  -> New every time

		Scoped     -> One per request

		Singleton  -> One for whole application
												
	
