	4. What is the difference between Scoped and Transient?

			Answer:

			Scoped creates one instance for the entire HTTP request.

			Transient creates a new instance every time it is injected.

			Example:

			Request
			   |
			Controller
			   |
			Service A
			   |
			Repository


			With Scoped:

			All classes receive the same object instance during the same HTTP request.


			With Transient:

			Each class gets a different object instance every time it is injected.
			
			
	5. Why is DbContext registered as Scoped?

		Answer:

		DbContext tracks entity changes during a request.
		Registering it as Scoped ensures all repositories and services in the same request share the same DbContext and transaction.
		Example:

		Update Product

			  ↓

		Update Stock

			  ↓

		Insert Audit Log

			  ↓

		SaveChanges() in the same time 


		All operations use one DbContext instance.
		
	6.  Why shouldn't DbContext be Singleton?

			Answer:

			DbContext should not be registered as Singleton because it is not thread-safe.

			A Singleton DbContext creates only one instance for the entire application.

			If multiple users use the application at the same time, they will share the same DbContext object, which can cause problems.

			Real-time Example:

			Imagine an online shopping website.

			User A:
			- Opens product page
			- Changes Product Name from "Laptop" to "Gaming Laptop"

			User B:
			- Opens the same product page
			- Changes Product Name from "Laptop" to "Office Laptop"


			If DbContext is Singleton:

			User A and User B use the same DbContext instance.

			Possible problems:

			- User A changes may affect User B changes
			- Entity tracking conflicts can happen
			- Wrong data may be saved
			- Application can throw exceptions
			
			
