8. Difference between Scoped and Singleton.
  Scoped creates one instance per HTTP request, while Singleton creates only one instance for the entire application lifetime.
Scoped is commonly used for services like DbContext because each request should have its own instance. Singleton is used for shared services like caching or configuration that can be reused across the application.
In short, Scoped = per request, Singleton = application lifetime.

9. What happens if a Singleton depends on a Scoped service?
  A Singleton service should not directly depend on a Scoped service because their lifetimes are different.
  A Singleton is created once for the entire application, while a Scoped service is created once per HTTP request. 
  If a Singleton holds a Scoped dependency, it may reuse the same scoped instance across multiple requests,
  causing incorrect behavior. ASP.NET Core throws an exception when it detects this. 
  The solution is to change the lifetime or create a scope manually using IServiceScopeFactory when required.
  
