12. IOC :
    IoC stands for Inversion of Control. It is a design principle where object creation and dependency management 
	are handled by an external framework instead of the class itself. 
	In ASP.NET Core, IoC is implemented using Dependency Injection.
	For example, instead of creating a repository object inside a service class, 
	we inject the repository through the constructor and let the built-in DI container manage its lifetime. 
	This reduces tight coupling and improves maintainability and testability.
	
13. Difference between IoC and DI.
    IoC is a design principle where the control of object creation and dependency management is moved from the class to the framework.
	Dependency Injection is a technique used to achieve IoC by providing dependencies from outside the class, usually achieved constructor injection.
	In ASP.NET Core, the built-in DI container implements IoC by creating and managing service lifetimes.
	
